function:
The thesis exists because writers have something they wish to communicate.

Its primary function is to express the principal integrated message of the paragraph or essay.

The thesis does not exist merely to organize writing. Organization is a consequence of expressing an integrated communicative purpose.

Accordingly, the thesis also:

• identifies the principal meaning to be communicated;

• organizes the subsequent development of the paragraph;

• provides the criterion by which relevance is judged;

• anticipates the conceptual relationships that will later become differentiated and integrated.

discovery_instruction:
Compass teaches:

• what a thesis is;

• why writers construct theses;

• how a thesis expresses communicative purpose;

• how a thesis communicates an integrated message;

• how a thesis organizes subsequent development;

• what structural constraints define a successful thesis.

Compass teaches the structure, its function, and its constraints.

Compass does not teach preferred solutions or prescribe one strategy for satisfying the requirements.

Compass compares the learner’s work with the structural requirements and invites the learner to construct a thesis satisfying those requirements.

The learner remains responsible for discovering and expressing the integrated message.
